Package: mos-kernel-virt-arm64
Version: @VERSION@
Architecture: @ARCH@
Maintainer: mos build <mos@example.invalid>
Section: kernel
Priority: optional
Description: mos kernel -- virt-arm64 (linux 6.12 LTS, configured for the verity boot)
 The Linux kernel the virt-arm64 board boots, built from the tag pinned in
 boards/virt-arm64/bsp/kernel/versions.env against the configuration recorded
 in boards/virt-arm64/bsp/kernel/config/. It ships /boot/vmlinuz-<release>, the
 /boot/config-<release> it was built from, and /usr/lib/modules/<release>/.
 .
 It is built here rather than taken from Debian, and the reason is the boot
 path rather than size. Debian's linux-image-arm64 has no CONFIG_DM_INIT, so
 the dm-mod.create= verity table on the kernel command line would be accepted
 and ignored, leaving no root at all -- and the usual repair is an initramfs
 that re-parses the line GRUB just wrote, which is a second implementation of
 the one boot contract. x64 shipped exactly that until PLAN-074. This kernel
 carries the device mapper, the dm-verity target and squashfs built in, so the
 root is assembled before the mount and the board ships no initrd.
 .
 It is NOT a generic distribution kernel and does not try to be, though it is
 larger than x64's: 9328 recorded config lines against that board's 4589,
 because arm64 has one `defconfig` covering every platform where x86 has
 x86_64_defconfig. What the fragment beside it ADDS is small and specific --
 virtio over PCIe for the root device and the network, the PL011 console the
 `virt` machine actually has, GPT and the FAT/ext4 filesystems the layout
 mounts. There is no wireless, sound, DRM or media stack in the image's
 userland to drive.
 .
 The artifact is arch/arm64/boot/Image; it is installed as vmlinuz-<release>
 because that is what boards/virt-arm64/board.env's SLOT_KERNEL_NAME says the
 boot partition carries, and the same grub.cfg shape x64 uses reads that name.
 .
 No Depends: the payload is a kernel image and its modules, and nothing in it
 links a shared library. It is not the boot chain either -- the firmware and
 GRUB provide that -- so nothing is enabled and nothing runs at install time.
